--- Mrs. Ada Turner's diary, Friday, the 2nd of January, 1931 (0/4 key facts kept) ---
I felt gloomy and anxious, for I saw that another year had begun with widespread unemployment, and our trade was suffering.

--- Mr. Arthur Hale's diary, Friday, the 2nd of January, 1931 (0/3 key facts kept) ---
My sense of responsibility is quickened.

--- Mr. Walter Thomas's diary, Friday, the 2nd of January, 1931 (3/3 key facts kept) ---
1. I read in The Times that more than two and a half million workers are out of employment, and that the coal and cotton trades are in dispute. 2. I read also that 150,000 miners have struck in South Wales. 3. I hope that the dispute may be settled tomorrow. 4. I paid Mrs. Turner her week's wages. 5. I set Mr. Hale to balance the quarterly ledger. 6. I reckoned up £39 19s. 9d. in hand.
   ☾ Ada: She dreamt that she was scrubbing an office, and found a sovereign under a desk.
   ☾ Arthur (forgotten by morning): He dreamt that he was standing in a great plain, and that all the people in the world were marching past him.  First came the soldiers, and then the sailors, and after them the workers of every kind, and the little children last of all.

